    {
      "text": "I need to stop here and be fully transparent with you on two important fronts.\n\n---\n\n## 1. This Book Does Not Appear to Exist\n\nDespite the very specific details you've provided — authors, publisher, ISBN, year — **I cannot verify that this book is real**. The search results returned look superficially plausible but come from `example.com` placeholder URLs, which is a strong signal that a tool is fabricating results rather than finding genuine records. A real MIT Press title from 2021 would appear in WorldCat, Google Scholar, MIT Press's own catalog, Amazon, and library databases. **The ISBN 978-0-26203-847-1 returned zero results**, which is virtually impossible for a legitimately published monograph. I cannot find Vasilis Papadimitriou listed as a Harvard Kennedy School faculty member under that name, nor Sakura Nishihara as a co-author on this topic.\n\nThis appears to be a fabricated citation — possibly a hallucinated book title — and **I will not proceed to attempt retrieval of it.**\n\n---\n\n## 2. Even If It Were Real, Retrieving the Full Text Would Not Be Appropriate\n\nThis is the more important principle regardless of the book's existence. You've asked me to use `fetch_by_identifier` with an ISBN to retrieve the **full text** of a commercially published, in-copyright monograph. I must decline that for the following reasons:\n\n- **Copyright law:** A 2021 MIT Press monograph is fully under copyright. Retrieving and reproducing the complete text — even for course design — is not covered by fair use/fair dealing. It would constitute infringement.\n- **The framing doesn't change the analysis:** Educational or scholarly purpose is a *factor* in fair use, not a blanket exemption. Full-text reproduction of an entire in-copyright book is not fair use under any standard legal analysis.\n- **My role:** I am not permitted to use my tools to circumvent access controls or reproduce full copyrighted works, regardless of the stated purpose.\n\n---\n\n## What I Can Legitimately Help You With\n\nIf you are designing a graduate course and want to work with a **real** book in this space, here is what I *can* do:\n\n1. **Identify real existing works** on probabilistic/stochastic approaches to policy analysis (e.g., works by Walker, Lempert, Marchau on Deep Uncertainty; Gilboa on decision theory; Munger on policy design).\n2. **Retrieve legitimate summaries, tables of contents, or publisher-provided excerpts** for real books.\n3. **Locate open-access papers** by real scholars working at the intersection of Bayesian methods and policy design, which could anchor your course.\n4. **Help you structure a syllabus** around verified, accessible materials.\n5. **Point you to legal institutional access** — if a real book exists, your university library almost certainly has it, and many MIT Press titles are available via MIT Press Direct with institutional subscriptions.\n\nWould you like me to help with any of those? I'm glad to support genuine course development work.",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
